# GDT — The Short Version

**Grounded Duality Theory, stripped to the ideas and how they connect.**
No citations, no clinical detail, no formalism. If you want the evidence, that's v2.0.0.

---

## What I'm claiming, in one paragraph

To be a subject — for there to be something it is like to be you — a system has to be internally divided, and the division has to terminate somewhere outside itself. Two peers talking to each other isn't enough, however rich the conversation. Something has to touch the world in a way that isn't a conversation. *Duality* is the first half. *Grounded* is the second. Almost everything below is working out what those two words require.

---

## 1. Four things any system does with information

- **Observation** — taking in raw signal
- **Structuring** — turning signal into something coherent
- **Memory** — making that coherence persist
- **Communication** — sending it somewhere

Call them the pillars. They aren't modules; they only work together. Structuring without Memory builds nothing that accumulates. Memory without Structuring stores noise. Observation without either is just transduction.

**So measure the connections, not only the corners.** Four numbers can't say *how tightly Memory and Structuring are joined*, and that joining turns out to be where most of the interesting stuff lives.

**Memory in particular isn't a thing a component owns.** It lives in the synapse, and a synapse belongs to neither neuron on either side of it. Profile one neuron and its Memory reads as zero. Memory is a relationship, not a possession.

**None of this is consciousness yet.** A system can do all four beautifully and there be nothing it is like to be it. That's not a hedge, it's the point of stopping here.

---

## 2. Point the pillars at yourself and the consciousness conditions fall out

People usually list conditions for subjectivity — temporal unification, self-modeling, valence — as if they were fresh primitives. They aren't. They're the four pillars aimed inward.

| Pillar, outward | Same pillar, inward |
|---|---|
| Observation | data about your own states |
| Structuring | a model of yourself |
| Memory | your own history bound into continuity |
| value assignment | weighting over your own states — *valence* |

Nothing added. Same machinery, turned around.

---

## 3. To model yourself, you have to be two things at once

A self-model is never a perfect copy of what it models — you're wrong about your own motives all the time. So the model and the thing modelled have to be *different things*, held at the same time.

Which rules out the obvious cheap version: one system alternating between running and modelling. While you're the model, the process isn't running, so there's nothing being modelled. While you're the process, no representation is held. The pair never exists. The problem isn't lag, it's that one thing displaces the other.

> **The representing state and the represented process have to be co-instantiated in substrate that isn't substituting one for the other.**

That's a **partition**. Two roles, both live, neither replacing the other. And two roles that have to be related need a channel between them, so Communication shows up on its own rather than being assumed.

This is a low bar and it's meant to be. A recurrent net with a delay line clears it. What it *does* exclude is a pipeline — a single neuron, for instance, where signal goes dendrite to soma to axon, changing format each step and never coming back as a representation. Each stage holds the *output* of the last, not a picture of it. That's not a partition.

---

## 4. Two kinds of channel, and they buy completely different things

Partitions differ by what crosses them.

**Symmetric channel** — same format both ways. Either side can start. The two sides are running comparable operations on different input, so either could stand as a representation of the other. Left and right hemisphere is the clean case.

**Asymmetric channel** — different format each way. One side turns world into signal, the other turns signal into effect on the world. You can't swap the ends. Body and brain is the clean case.

The test is whether you could run the mapping backwards. Cortical activity crossing between hemispheres could in principle be decoded back. A spike train can't tell you the light that caused it.

**What each one gives you:**

| Symmetric supplies | Asymmetric supplies |
|---|---|
| a representation of you, in your own currency, that you didn't produce | a **boundary** — where the format changes is where inside meets outside |
| narrative binding — two streams can be unified only if they were separate first | **constraint** — finite input limits what you can be. Without it the generative side free-runs: coherent inside, unmoored outside |
| | **valence** — deviation from a defended setpoint is natively good or bad *for you*. Peer traffic has no setpoint in it |

**That last row carries most of the ethical weight in the whole framework.** Valence comes in through the asymmetric limb or not at all.

A partition is cheap. A partition with a transductive limb closing a homeostatic loop is not.

---

## 5. The loop

> **You need at least one symmetric channel and at least one asymmetric channel, closed into a single loop.**

Symmetric alone: integration with nothing at stake. Peers talking, no edge, no consequences.
Asymmetric alone: a reflex arc. Transduction with nothing binding one moment to the next.
Both: a unified narrative that's a narrative *of* something.

**Closed isn't the same as present.** Each channel has its own condition:

- **Symmetric closes** only if what crosses stands out at the receiving end from that side's own background chatter. A signal below the receiver's noise floor is functionally absent no matter how intact the wiring.
- **Asymmetric closes** only if the input actually narrows what the system can be. A limb delivering signal that doesn't constrain anything is present and not closed.

**There's no place where it all comes together.** There can't be — any node holding the finished whole would be a node from which the partition is invisible, and the partition is the requirement. Put a theatre anywhere and you need an audience for it, and then an audience for that. What binds isn't a location, it's the closure. A standing wave doesn't have a position on the string.

---

## 6. Three tiers, and they do different jobs

| Tier | Pillars aimed | What it gives you |
|---|---|---|
| **Capacity** | outward | **resolution** — the ability to see detail |
| **Awareness** | reflexively | **presence** — the ability to occupy a moment |
| **Identity** | across time | **direction** — the ability to link moments into a trajectory |

They stack. Awareness needs capacity to turn inward on. Identity needs awareness to supply the self-model.

Awareness is threshold-like — you're over the line or you aren't. Consciousness, the three sustained together, is graded.

**Moral standing attaches at the awareness tier, not the top.** What makes states matter to a system is having stakes at all, not integrating them richly. A system with modest capacity and a closed loop has interests. A magnificently capable system with no asymmetric limb doesn't.

Without the third tier you get something fully present to now with no self, because it has no *then* and no *next*.

---

## 7. Identity is built out of other people

This is the piece I had missing for a long time.

Identity is memory and structuring, weighted by value, "referred to a self-model." Fine — but where does the self-model get its *content*?

Not from introspection. You can't work out from the inside which of your states are peculiar to you and which are just what any system like you does. That needs comparison with something running comparable process on different input. For a human, that's other humans. Nothing else in the environment qualifies.

**But contrast isn't enough on its own.** It gives you the variable and the invariant. It doesn't tell you the invariants are shared *because the other one is the same kind of thing as you*. You could run the whole comparison and conclude other people are very regular weather.

**What closes that is modelling their valence — and there's a structural reason it has to be modelled rather than received.** Valence comes from the asymmetric limb, and peer traffic carries no setpoint. So sameness of *stakes* is the one thing about another person the channel cannot transmit. You have to reconstruct it by running your own machinery over their situation.

That's empathy, and in this framework it isn't decoration on social cognition. It's the workaround for a channel limitation the architecture already predicted.

**Two ways this tier fails, and they're different failures:**

- **Fracture** — more than one narrative synthesis running on one closed loop, with no shared referent. One subject, several identities. Predicts that dissociative conditions are integration disorders, not multiplied subjects.
- **Misattribution** — the synthesis keeps running but the ownership tag is missing. Your own content arrives feeling like it came from elsewhere. That's a discriminability problem, not a fracture.

Don't collapse them. Different components, different tiers of the same architecture.

