## 9. Grounded duality vs facilitated duality

The newest piece, and the one I'd press hardest on.

Some constructed systems really do satisfy the architecture. A corporation has transducers of its own — accounting, audit, instrumentation. It has a partition: the accounting represents operations while operations runs. It defends a boundary, resists acquisition, sheds parts under scarcity to protect the core. And it can register error no individual member registers, which is the thing that separates a real system from a description of one.

So I can't wave it away. But it isn't the same kind of thing as a body.

> **Grounded** — the limb terminates in a boundary the system didn't choose and can't revise. Its transducers are inherited.
>
> **Facilitated** — the limb terminates in transducers the system *designed* and can redesign. Its grounding is **borrowed** from the grounded systems that built it.

It's the knock-off version. It functions. It gets the job done. It is not a replacement, and the reason is that it lacks something the original never had to acquire.

**The consequences:**

- Corporate constraint is real and *on loan*. Every corporate transducer traces back to a person with a body who can't decide to stop being hungry. Cut that chain and the loop opens.
- **Goodhart's law falls out as a prediction.** A constraining structure the generative side can rewrite is a constraint in name only, so organizations drift toward measuring what they can hit rather than what constrains them. Not a quirk of management — architecture.
- A firm under hard external constraint it *didn't write* is more closed than an unconstrained one. Which is the Preface again: the division has to terminate outside the dividing system.
- **Stakes require grounded closure.** Facilitated structures have borrowed stakes — the stakes of the people constituting them, aggregated and refracted. That's why harm through a corporation is real harm without the corporation having interests of its own.
- Nations sit between the poles. Territory and population can't be redesigned; most of what a state measures itself against can be.

**A related distinction that matters:** you can *extend* a setpoint — come to defend a child, a project, a community — and the loop stays closed. You can't *revise* one — decide to stop registering hunger. Extension adds what you defend. Revision replaces it, and a setpoint you can replace isn't constraining you.

