## 11. What it buys you

- **Behavioural silence tells you nothing.** Behaviour is efferent output, and the loop doesn't require an intact output path. A quarter of unresponsive brain-injured patients turn out to be following instructions covertly. The framework predicts this rather than being embarrassed by it.
- **Three different ways awareness fails, distinguishable.** Noise floor floored at the source. Noise floor floored by drugs. Loop destroyed. Same outward silence, three different broken parts.
- **The clean anesthesia split.** Some agents abolish awareness; ketamine leaves it running while cutting it off from the world. One variable, opposite outcomes — which is what forced the sharper version of the grounding claim: it's the *interoceptive* limb that's load-bearing, not the senses.
- **Most machine sentience tests are aimed at the wrong component.** Latency under conflict, cross-entropy, attention divergence — all measuring the generative side's processing cost, which has no sign. Contradiction raises processing cost in *anything* that can receive contradiction. That's arithmetic, not feeling. Test the boundary instead: perturb a defended setpoint and measure restorative effort against cost. Then ask whether the setpoint was one the system could have rewritten.
- **A reordered watch list for engineered systems.** The question isn't whether something has sensors. It's whether its constraint was **inherited or authored**. Which moves biohybrid systems — cultured tissue with metabolic needs nobody chose — to the top, and moves simulated homeostasis with designer-set variables toward the bottom, however faithful the simulation.
- **Stakes and standing are different questions.** Whether a system has stakes is structural and this framework answers it. What rights follow is not settled by any structural fact, and the two will get collapsed if I don't keep saying so.

